Measure the two-day stop limit in wait_until() from the start of the stop.
The limit is on the clock of the whole dive, so once a dive passes 48 hours every
call returns 50 hours, which is earlier than the clock. plan() takes the difference
as a negative stop time and doubles it into the next call's leap, and from there
wait_until() recurses without end until the stack is gone: a 300 m trimix plan
under VPM-B crashes the app. Nothing changes for a dive shorter than two days. A
stop of two days still comes back as a stop of more than two days, which is what
plan() reports as PLAN_ERROR_TIMEOUT. Report upstream.

--- a/core/planner.cpp
+++ b/core/planner.cpp
@@ -585,8 +585,8 @@
 static int wait_until(struct deco_state *ds, struct dive *dive, int clock, int min, int leap, int stepsize, depth_t depth, depth_t target_depth, depth_t avg_depth, int bottom_time, struct gasmix gasmix, int po2, double surface_pressure, enum divemode_t divemode)
 {
 	// When a deco stop exceeds two days, there is something wrong...
-	if (min >= 48 * 3600)
-		return 50 * 3600;
+	if (min - clock >= 48 * 3600)
+		return clock + 50 * 3600;
 	// Round min + leap up to the next multiple of stepsize
 	int upper = min + leap + stepsize - 1 - (min + leap - 1) % stepsize;
 	// Is the upper boundary too small?
